# The Smallest Livelit That Needs Splices #

# Every livelit in this deck so far holds DATA. Tree Care's model is a #
# tree; the color picker's is three numbers. A model is written into #
# the program text, so it has to be plain serializable data. #

# That observation is true. The conclusion usually drawn from it is #
# NOT, and this slide used to draw it. Worth correcting carefully, #
# because it changes what splices are actually for. #

# WHAT ALREADY WORKS #

# Here is a slider reporting a number between two bounds. Drag it. #

let ^between = {
  # pct is where the handle sits, 0 to 100. lo and hi are the ends of #
  # the range. #
  # A spliced field carries a HANDLE to the client's code as well as #
  # what it evaluates to. Figure 3 puts the handle in the model; the #
  # value rides along because splices are transparent, which is the one #
  # thing we have that the paper does not. #
  type Model = (
    pct=Int,
    lo=(ref=SpliceRef, value=Int),
    hi=(ref=SpliceRef, value=Int)
  );

  # Only the handle moves. There is no SetLo or SetHi, and that is the #
  # point: the bounds belong to whoever is USING this livelit, not to #
  # the livelit. See "who owns what" below. #
  type Action = + Slide(Int);

  type Expansion = Int;

  # Parenthesizing a field's value marks it as a SPLICE -- a region of #
  # the client's own program, held inside the widget. Everything below #
  # explains what that buys. #
  # No `: Model` here. init supplies VALUES; the refs do not exist yet -- #
  # nothing has made a splice at this point, and making one is an effect, #
  # which is exactly what Figure 3's new_splice is and we do not have. #
  let init = (pct=50, lo=(0), hi=(100));

  let update = fun (m, a) : (Model, Action) ->
    case a
    | Slide(p) => (pct=p, lo=m.lo, hi=m.hi)
    end;

  # Where the handle lands, in the client's units. #
  let at = fun m : Model ->
    m.lo.value + (m.hi.value - m.lo.value) * m.pct / 100;

  let view = fun m : Model ->
    Html.div(
      [Attr.style([("display", "flex"), ("gap", "0.4em"),
                   ("align-items", "center"),
                   ("font-family", "var(--code-font)"),
                   ("font-size", "0.8em")])],
      # Each splice is named by the ref its own field carries, not by #
      # a position: Html.splice(m.lo.ref), never Html.splice(0). The #
      # view says WHICH splice it means, so it no longer has to agree #
      # with the model about counting order. #
      # They are real editors: click in and type. What you type is code #
      # in YOUR scope, not in the livelit's. #
      [Html.splice(m.lo.ref),
       Html.input(
         [Attr.type_("range"), Attr.min("0"), Attr.max("100"),
          Attr.value(string_of_int(m.pct)),
          Attr.on_input(fun s -> Slide(int_of_string(s)))]),
       Html.splice(m.hi.ref),
       Html.text(" = " ++ string_of_int(at(m)))]);

  let expand_fun = fun m : Model -> at(m);

  # Width of the widget's OWN chrome only -- the slider and the " = N". #
  # The splices add their own width on top, because only the editor knows #
  # how long the client's code is. Counting them here too double-reserves #
  # and the widget sits in a wide empty box. 22 was too tight and #
  # clipped the " = N"; 26 leaves room for a three-digit result. #
  let shape = Inline(26)
} in

# THE CORRECTION #

# The claim this slide used to make was that a bound depending on the #
# client's code has nowhere to live -- that `baseline * 2` is an #
# expression, not data, so a model cannot hold it. #

# That is false, and it was false before any of the splice work. A #
# livelit's model argument is an ordinary expression in the client's #
# scope. Write a variable into it and it resolves. The use below does #
# exactly that, and it would have evaluated correctly with no splices #
# at all. #

# So splices are not what makes `baseline * 2` POSSIBLE. #

# WHAT SPLICES ACTUALLY CHANGE #

# Three things, none of which is expressiveness: #

#   LOCATION. Without a splice the bound is program text sitting #
#   beside the widget. With one it is inside the widget's own view, #
#   where the author put it. #

#   PRESENTATION. The author decides where the client's code appears, #
#   what sits next to it, and what it is labeled. A livelit used to #
#   control every part of its view except the one part that was code. #

#   OWNERSHIP. There is now a region of the program the CLIENT owns #
#   inside a widget the AUTHOR wrote. That is why Action has no SetLo: #
#   `update` cannot write a spliced field, by construction. A livelit #
#   may READ the client's code; it may not rewrite it. #

# The honest one-line version: #

#   A livelit could always COMPUTE with the client's code. #
#   It could not HOLD any. #

# READING AND SHOWING AT THE SAME TIME #

# Note what `at` does. It reaches for m.lo.value and m.hi.value while #
# the view reaches for m.lo.ref -- the same field, read as a number in #
# one place and as a handle in the other. So the widget RENDERS the #
# client's code as an editor and USES its value in the same view. #
# That falls out of splices being transparent to statics: the model #
# argument still evaluates normally. #

# The paper needs a separate eval_splice for this. Here it is free, #
# because the splice never stopped being part of the expression. #

let baseline = 10 in

let shown = ^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(baseline), hi=(baseline * 2)))) in

# SPLICES COMPOSE #

# A splice holds an EXPRESSION, and a use of a livelit is an expression. #
# So one of these can be the bound of another. Three ways, and the #
# difference between them is only where you write it. #

# 1. BY NAME. The inner slider's value is the outer slider's maximum. #

let cap = ^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(0), hi=(200)))) in
let by_name = ^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(0), hi=(cap)))) in

# 2. NESTED IN PLACE. The same relationship, with the inner slider #
# living INSIDE the outer one's max splice. Drag the inner handle and #
# the outer slider's range changes under you. #

let nested =
  ^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(0),
    hi=(^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(0), hi=(200)))))))) in

# 3. COMPUTED. A splice is not a slot for a widget, it is a region of #
# your program -- so the maximum can be arithmetic over one. #

let computed =
  ^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(0),
    hi=((^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(0), hi=(200))))) * 2 + 10)))) in

# Watch where the parens close in 3. Written `hi=(slider) * 2 + 10` the #
# marker takes in only the slider and leaves the arithmetic outside, so #
# the field has no ref and does not typecheck. The parens say exactly #
# which text becomes yours to edit. #

(shown, by_name, nested, computed)

# Try: change baseline to 20 and watch both bounds follow. Or type #
# `baseline * 3` into the right-hand splice. Or empty a bound and see #
# what the widget does with a hole. #

# ================= WHAT THIS IS NOT ================= #

# This is NOT the paper's SpliceRef, and the difference is worth being #
# precise about (Omar et al., PLDI 2021, Figure 3). #

# THE MODEL DOES HOLD REFS -- AND ALSO THE VALUES #

# Figure 3 has: #
#   type Model = (pct=Int, lo=SpliceRef, hi=SpliceRef) #

# Here it is a pair of both: #
#   lo=(ref=SpliceRef, value=Int) #

# The ref is real, and it is what `view` names. The VALUE riding #
# alongside is the shortcut. The paper cannot do that -- an expansion #
# has no way to see inside a hole, which is why reading one is a #
# command, eval_splice. Splices here are transparent to statics, so #
# the content is already typed in your scope and already evaluates in #
# place, and the value comes along for free. #

# The cost of the shortcut is that there is no way to say a bound has #
# NO value. eval_splice answers Maybe(Result), with Indet for an #
# expression that will not reduce, and the widget decides what to #
# show. Delete a bound here and the widget cannot decide anything -- #
# the result simply renders as a hole, because nothing asked it. #

# THERE IS NO new_splice #

#   new_splice : (Typ, Maybe(Exp)) -> UpdateCmd(SpliceRef) #

# The paper MAKES a splice as an effect, at a declared type. Here #
# splices are recovered from the program text on every load, by the #
# parens the author wrote. Nothing types the hole at a declared type; #
# it is typed only by where it sits in the model tuple. For `between` #
# that happens to give the right answer, Int, but that is luck of #
# position, not a mechanism. #

# You can see the gap in `init` above: it has no `: Model` annotation, #
# because init supplies VALUES and only the use site supplies REFS. #
# Nothing has made a splice when init is written. Making one is an #
# effect -- which is exactly what new_splice is, and why the paper #
# makes init a command rather than a value. #

# The other consequence: the number of splices is fixed by the text. A #
# livelit cannot make one at runtime, so an editable LIST of splices -- #
# the obvious next example -- is not expressible. #

# EXPANSION DOES NOT RETURN SPLICES #

#   expand_mac : Model -> (Exp, List(SpliceRef)) #

# The paper's expand_mac writes a PROGRAM, handing back the splices #
# alongside, with the quoted expression a FUNCTION of them: #

#   (`fun lo hi -> lo + (hi - lo) * pct / 100`, [m.lo, m.hi]) #

# That shape is not decoration. It is how the paper gets CAPTURE #
# AVOIDANCE: a splice passed as an argument is evaluated OUTSIDE the #
# expansion, so a binding inside the expansion cannot capture it. #

# Our expand_fun returns a VALUE, from bounds already evaluated #
# in the client's scope. Capture cannot arise because nothing is #
# substituted into a binder -- but equally, nothing here demonstrates #
# the paper's solution. We avoided the problem rather than solving it. #

# WHAT WOULD BREAK FIRST #

# Two known walls, in the order you would hit them: #

#   A splice's content is lost through a save/load round trip -- a #
#   splice prints as nothing but its content. Only the parens survive, #
#   which is why they are the marker and why init rebuilds the splices #
#   on every load. #

#   Committing a model rebuilds the zipper from the ROOT, so a widget #
#   action destroys the editor the caret was sitting in. Nothing about #
#   a splice survives that on its own; focus is put back afterwards, #
#   explicitly, by matching the splice id (FocusEffect.keep_splice_focus). #
#   That restoration is not complete -- it is a patch over a rebuild, #
#   not a caret the rebuild preserves. #

# Both are honest consequences of building splices out of the editor's #
# existing machinery instead of the paper's. Closing them is what #
# SpliceRef is actually for. #
